# Bearer token check

The nx-fhir `auth-resource` feature adds a bearer token check to this server. The check applies to requests under `/fhir`. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, a request needs a JSON Web Token (JWT) from the issuer of `app.auth.issuer`, other than the requests in [Request rule](#request-rule) that pass with no token.

## What the feature adds

The feature adds `BearerTokenInterceptor`, `AuthResourceProperties`, `AuthResourceConfig`, `IssuerMetadata`, `FhirBaseUrl`, `TrustAllSslContext`, and `WellKnownInterceptor` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/`, and the unit tests `BearerTokenInterceptorTest` and `FhirBaseUrlTest`, the test helpers `TestCertificates` and `TestIssuer`, and the integration test `AuthResourceIT`, which runs under `mvn verify`, under `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/`.

The install writes `app.auth.enabled: false` to `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` and to `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml`, so a test runs with the check only when it sets `app.auth.enabled` to `true`.

## Configuration

The install replaces `app.auth` with one map: `enabled: true`, `issuer`, `audience` when the option has a value, `public-endpoints: []`, `ssl-verify`, and the comment line of `bypass-header`. The keys:

- `app.auth.enabled`: `true` makes the server check each request under `/fhir`, other than the requests in [Request rule](#request-rule) that pass with no token, and `app.auth.issuer` must then have a value, or the application stops at startup. `false` or absent: the server has no check and ignores the other `app.auth` properties.
- `app.auth.issuer`: the `iss` claim of a token must be equal to it. The default issuer of the generator is `https://udap-security.fast.hl7.org`, the public FAST security server.
- `app.auth.audience`: optional. When it is set, the `aud` claim must contain it.
- `app.auth.jwks-uri`: optional. When it is set, the server reads the signing keys from this URL and reads no discovery document. Use it for an issuer whose `iss` value is not a URL that the server can reach.
- `app.auth.public-endpoints`: a list of entries relative to the FHIR base. A request that fits an entry passes with no token, for each request method. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, an entry in a form other than the three below stops the application at startup.
- `app.auth.ssl-verify`: `true` or absent makes the server check the certificate chain and the host name for each call that the auth features make. `false` turns off these checks, and with `app.auth.enabled: true` the server writes one WARN line at startup. The install writes `ssl-verify: false` when the issuer is an `https` URL with the host `localhost`, and `ssl-verify: true` for each other issuer.
- `app.auth.bypass-header`: optional, a header name. A request that has this header, with any value, skips the check. When it is absent or empty, the server has no bypass. When it is set and `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, the server writes one WARN line at startup that names the header. The install writes the comment line `# bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth`, and the bypass is off. To turn on the bypass, remove `# ` from that line.

The three forms of a public endpoint:

- `Type`, for example `Practitioner`. Each request for that resource type passes, also a create, an update, a delete, and an operation on the type or on an instance of the type. Such a request can return resources of other types. With the entry `Patient`, a search with `_include` or `_revinclude` returns resources of other types, and `Patient/$everything` returns the record of each patient.
- `$operation`, for example `$bulk-publish`. A request for that server-level operation passes.
- `Type/$operation`, for example `Patient/$match`. A request for that operation on the type or on an instance of the type passes.

The server compares an entry with the resource type and the operation name that HAPI FHIR reads from the request.

WARNING: With `app.auth.ssl-verify: false`, the server accepts any certificate from the hosts it calls. Use it only with a local development certificate.

WARNING: With `app.auth.bypass-header` set, each caller that sends this header skips the token check. Remove the property for a deployment that has data that is not public.

When `jwks-uri` is absent, the server reads `{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration` and uses its `jwks_uri`. The `issuer` member of that document must be equal to `app.auth.issuer`. The server reads the document at the first token and keeps it after a successful read. After a failed read, it reads the document again at the next token. The server starts and runs while the issuer is down. The discovery read and the key set read each have a connect timeout of 5 seconds and a read timeout of 10 seconds.

## Request rule

The server applies the first rule that fits. A request that passes by rules 1 to 5 has no claims entry.

1. `GET` and `HEAD` requests to `/fhir/metadata` pass.
2. A request to a path under `/fhir/.well-known/` passes.
3. A request that fits an entry of `app.auth.public-endpoints` passes.
4. A request for a later page of a search or of the result of an operation (`?_getpages=<id>`) passes with no token, also with an expired or a wrong token. The id is a random value that the server gives in the `next` link of the page before it. So a page link works for each caller who has it, also for a different client and after the token expired. HAPI FHIR gives a server-level request with a `_getpages` value to its paging handler, also when the path names an operation.
5. A request that has the bypass header passes.
6. Each other request needs the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
7. The server validates the signature, the issuer, the `exp` claim, the `nbf` claim when present, and the audience when configured. The `exp` claim is necessary. The checks of `exp` and `nbf` allow a clock difference of 60 seconds. The token must use RS256, RS384, ES256, or ES384. When the token has a `typ` header, it must be `JWT` or `at+jwt`.
8. After a valid token, the server puts the verified claims in the user data of the request.

The OpenAPI pages of the server answer with no token when `hapi.fhir.openapi_enabled` is `true`, the value in the `application.yaml` of the starter. `GET /fhir/api-docs` returns the OpenAPI document of the server as YAML, `GET /fhir/swagger-ui/` returns the Swagger UI page, and `GET /fhir/` with `Accept: text/html` redirects to `/fhir/swagger-ui/`.

## Responses

- No token: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer`.
- A token that fails: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"`. The server log has the reason in one INFO line.
- The server cannot check the token: status 503 with no `WWW-Authenticate` header. This occurs when the read of the discovery document or of the key set fails, when the discovery document names a different issuer or has no `jwks_uri`, when `app.auth.jwks-uri` is not a valid URL, or when the key source refuses a new read of the key set. The key source allows two reads of the key set in 30 seconds. The server log has the reason in one WARN line.
- The body is an `OperationOutcome` with a fixed text. Its issue code is `security` for status 401 and `transient` for status 503.

## Claims in a custom interceptor

The check runs at the HAPI FHIR pointcut `SERVER_INCOMING_REQUEST_PRE_HANDLER_SELECTED`. A hook at a later pointcut reads the verified claims with `requestDetails.getUserData().get(BearerTokenInterceptor.CLAIMS_KEY)`, an unmodifiable `Map<String, Object>` under the key `auth.claims`. For a request that passed with no token, the value is `null`.
